# answer
## Ten o'clock
It is the first Friday in March, and at ten o'clock the knocker goes, three taps and then one. It is the same man, the gray headed servant in the blue coat, and he has the same card, and he has a good deal more patience than anyone should be asked for. He says, to the air above Aurélien's shoulder, that Monsieur Beaumont is at home, and will be pleased to see Monsieur Colline at supper, and has sent the carriage.
Nobody in the house is able to speak. The old aunt, from the stair, says only that it is as well to go, since it is a thing that is going to be done in any case, and that a man should walk through the front door of a house like that on his own feet, with his hat in his hand, and not be fetched.
$ _cravat = "One of them"
?if _lead != ""
  $ _cravat = name(_lead)
?end
?if coven_size() > 0 and b4.circle == "ahead"
  The others came up the river in the second week of February, in crates marked as pianos, and are in the loft above the printing office, where they have been very well behaved. They watch you dress with a kind of grave, family attention. {_cravat} does your cravat, which you have not asked for, and tells you that you are to remember that he is a host, and that a host is a man who has already decided.
?elif coven_size() > 0 and b4.circle == "all"
  They stand in the hall, in the lamplight, the whole small family of you, and watch you go down the steps to the carriage with expressions that you could not have described a century ago, and can now. You are not going alone. You are going, you understand, with all of them behind you, and you are the only one who is to be seen.
?elif b4.circle == "stay"
  You go alone. It is a thing that you have done a very great many times, and it has never before seemed quite so much like a decision.
?end
The carriage goes out of the city by the Bayou Road, in the dark, between the live oaks and the low, wet fields, and there is a moment when you pass out of the lamps and the whole of the night closes in, green and soft and loud with frogs. You find that you are listening, with a part of yourself that has not been used in a long time, for the others. It is the oldest sense there is. It is the one by which a wolf knows another wolf.
They are there. There are seven of them, in a house two miles off, and they know that you are coming, and they are very much awake.
<~

# supper
## The river house
$ do chart("neworleans.beaumont")
It stands at the end of an avenue of oaks, under a low roof that sweeps down over a gallery on every side, and it is lit in every window, which a house of that kind does not do for a guest. There are columns, and a hall that goes through the whole of the building from front to back with the doors open on a garden, and a smell of beeswax and jasmine and old cypress. The servants wear blue. They are all, you notice, very old.
Augustin Beaumont is waiting in the hall. He is tall, and silver at the temple, and perfectly still, in a black coat of the cut of thirty years ago, and he comes forward with both hands out and a smile of entire and unforced warmth. It is the smile of a man who has never in his life had to wonder whether he would be received. "Monsieur Colline," he says. "You are exactly as late as I should have wished. It is a very long way from Paris."
He takes your hand. It is a plain handshake, and it goes on a moment too long, and in that moment you feel him look. It is not a look of the eyes. It is a pressure, patient and courteous and a century old, like a hand laid flat on a door, and you find that you are leaning on it, very gently, with all of your weight, and that it does not move.
He is a good deal more careful afterward. "Ah," he says softly, and lets go.
?if b4.circle == "all" or (b4.circle == "ahead" and coven_size() > 0)
  He has also, you realize, felt the others, a long way off in the town, as a man feels a draft from a door that he knows he did not leave open. His eyes go, for a moment, toward the city. He does not say anything about it. He is much too well bred.
?end
There are six at the table. There is his lady, Isaure, who is small and dark and exquisite in gray, and looks at you as a woman looks at a wine she has been told about. There is a pair of brothers in their forties, in evening dress, who sit with their elbows exactly alike. There is a thin, quiet woman who is introduced as the widow Marchand, and says nothing, and eats nothing, and never once takes her eyes off your hands. There is a gentleman of a great age, in a high collar, who was, he tells you, in the Battle of New Orleans, and in a good many more recent affairs, and has been asleep since dinner.
And there is a young man at the foot of the table, handsome, flushed, with his father's mouth and none of his father's patience, who is not introduced at all, and is Lucien. He raises his glass to you, very slightly, with a smile that does not reach anywhere. "The sixth," he says. "I had imagined a taller man."
$ do meet("lucien")
"Lucien," says Beaumont, without turning his head, and the young man's glass goes down.
There is a dinner. It is a very good one, in the manner of the country, with turtle soup and a pompano and a daube, and wine from the cellars of three generations, and a pitcher of something dark, from the kitchen, that is brought in covered and set at each place and is not mentioned. The talk is of the weather, and the river, and the price of sugar, and the Opera, and a great many people who have been dead for some time. It is all done with such perfect and easy courtesy that it is some while before you notice that you are being very gently and very thoroughly taken apart.
They know the name. They know a good deal else. They know that the house of Valère has had a quiet friend for sixty years. They know, to the month, when you came ashore. They do not know where you lie by day, and are courteous enough, you notice, not to ask. At one point the old man wakes, and looks down the table, and says in a clear voice, "He has the Roman face. I said that he would," and goes back to sleep.
Over the coffee, Beaumont leans back, and puts his fingertips together, and says that he would like to say a thing, if Monsieur Colline will permit it, that is not a pleasant thing to hear.
"There were six of us," he says. "In the beginning. It was told to my grandfather's sire, in Lyon, in a year when it was very much less dangerous to say it, that there were six bloods and that the sixth had gone out of the world. We have kept the place at the table. It is," and he smiles, "a very small matter. A chair. But we have kept it for a hundred years in a country where nobody keeps anything, and I am a little moved, I find, to see it filled."
:the_table
* Take the chair, and thank him for the keeping of it
  $ b4.supper = "guest"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  You say that you are honored, and that you did not know, and that you will be very much in his debt. It is, you find, nearly true. Isaure smiles, a quick small genuine smile, and Beaumont inclines his head, with the grace of a man receiving a thing that he was always going to be given. It is a very comfortable moment. You are aware that it is also the moment at which a man makes a mistake.
* Answer him as an equal, and name what you are
  $ b4.supper = "equal"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing += 2
  You say that you are old, and that you are the Roman's, and the Roman's maker's, and that you were at the burning of a very great many things, and that you have been dead a good deal longer than the Compact has been alive. You say it quite gently. It is the first time in the evening that anyone at the table has looked at you without calculation. The brothers have put down their cups. Beaumont says, softly, "Yes. I had hoped it was so," and you have the strong impression that he has been waiting a very long time to be frightened of somebody properly.
* Say that you came to the city to be quiet, and ask that he permit it
  $ b4.supper = "stranger"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ standing -= 1
  You say that you are tired, and that you came to the country for the air, and that you desire nothing in it but to be left to your accounts. It is a very reasonable speech, and Beaumont hears it through with great and sympathetic attention, and at the end of it says, "But of course," in the voice of a man who has been told by a child that it does not want its supper. Lucien laughs, under his breath. Isaure looks at her plate.
<~

# compact
## The Compact of the River
They take him, when the table is cleared, into a long room at the back of the house, with books to the ceiling and a shaded lamp and a window on the garden. It is a room that smells of paper and old tobacco. There is a table, and on the table a sheet, in a brown ink that has been mended in places, in a French of the last century. At the foot of it are six names, in six hands, and a blank place under them.
"The Compact of the River," says Beaumont. "We do not call it a law. It is only what is done." He reads it aloud, very simply, as a man reads a grace. A house does not turn another in the other's parish without leave. A house does not feed upon another's people, nor take its sleep within its walls. A quarrel is not carried out of doors. The houses stand together against any stranger who would make a noise. And the house of the sixth blood, when it is asked, will stand at the river's side.
$ do discover("the_compact_of_the_river")
It is, you think, a very fair thing, as these things go, and a very old one, and written in a hand that you can trust. It has one clause in it that you read three times. It does not say what the river's side is. It says only that it will be asked.
"It has been signed by every house in the lower valley for ninety years," says Beaumont. "It has never yet been asked of anyone to do anything harder than not to be rude. I do not suppose it will be. But I am a prudent man, and I like to know where my neighbors stand."
:the_pen
* Sign it
  $ b4.compact = "signed"
  $ standing += 2
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ humanity -= 1
  The pen is a plain steel pen, and the ink is brown, and the name goes down in your own hand, in the Roman letters of your youth, under the other six. You feel it settle on you, as a roof settles on a house, with a small stir of the air. It is a thing that is done. It is going to be a very great deal harder to undo than it was to do. Beaumont blows on the ink, with care, and looks at it for some time.
* Sign it, but write that the river's side is to be named in the asking
  $ b4.compact = "bargained"
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing += 1
  You write it in the margin, in your best clear hand, and it is a very short line. It says that the river's side shall be named by the one who asks it, in plain words, before it is answered. Beaumont reads it twice. He is silent a long time. At the end of it he laughs, a real laugh, an old and rather surprised one, and says that nobody has put a word in the margin of the Compact since the year seventy two, and that he thinks it a very good word, and that Lucien will not like it at all. He signs beside you, to witness it.
* Decline, and say that you will keep to it without signing
  $ b4.compact = "refused"
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ standing -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  You say that you have signed things for a great many people, and that it has not, in your experience, made them kinder. You will keep to every word of it, you say, as a guest keeps to the customs of a house. You will simply not put your name on it. There is a long, still, polite silence. Beaumont says only, "I see," and rolls the sheet with great gentleness, and it is the gentleness that tells you what you have done.
  Lucien, who has been standing in the door, smiles. It is the first honest thing that he has done all night.
Beaumont sees you to the carriage himself. He stands a moment under the lamp, bareheaded, in the moths, with his hand on the door. "I am told," he says, "that there are others of your house. In the city. A family." He lets it lie. "It is a great thing to have a family. I should be sorry if anything were to trouble it. We are very quiet here, Monsieur Colline, but we are not at all careless of our neighbors." It is courteously said. It is also, you are fairly sure, a perfectly exact description of a knife.
<~

# hunger
## The road home
* Take what the carriage horses will spare, in the dark of the avenue
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  It is not much, and the coachman is a long way up, and thinking of his bed. The horse stands and shakes, and is a good deal calmer than you are. You leave a coin on the seat, from habit, though no one has ever once sent you a bill.
* Send word to the Rue Royale, and have a donor brought to the carriage
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ do earn(-2)
  A boy is sent ahead on a mule. By the time you are in the Faubourg there is a woman at the gate with a shawl over her head, and a face that tells you nothing, and a price already agreed. It is done in the carriage, with the blinds drawn. She counts the money twice, carefully, and says goodnight in a voice that is polite and a good way off.
* Stop the carriage in the Faubourg, and find someone in the dark
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  There is a man who has been a long time in a tavern, and who is going home by the wall, unsteadily, with a song. It is over very quickly. You lay him in the lee of a doorway, with his hat over his face, and he will wake in the morning with a headache and a firm conviction that he has been robbed. You feel entirely well, and not at all like yourself.
<~

