# gifts_night
## What the family can do
The long room has a courtyard behind it, high-walled and open to the stars, and you take the family out into it, one by one, in the hour before the dawn, and ask each of them to show you.
?if has_gift("old_blood")
  It is the old blood, you understand, that they are showing you, whatever else they do. Each of them is stronger than a fledgling of that age has any right to be, and it is yours, and they know it, and nobody says so.
?end
?if gift_holder("strength") != ""
  {name(gift_holder("strength"))} lifts a cart axle out of the dirt with one hand and sets it down again with an apology to the flagstones.
?end
?if gift_holder("charm") != ""
  {name(gift_holder("charm"))} asks the cook, without raising {pg(gift_holder("charm"), "his", "her", "their")} voice, for a thing that the cook does not at all wish to give, and the cook gives it, and is puzzled for an hour afterward.
?end
?if gift_holder("shadows") != ""
  {name(gift_holder("shadows"))} crosses the courtyard in plain view of three people, and not one of them, afterward, can say that anyone crossed it.
?end
?if gift_holder("merchants_eye") != ""
  {name(gift_holder("merchants_eye"))} looks at the cook's account book for the length of a breath, and says what the cook is cheating the grocer out of, and why, and what to do about it that will do no harm.
?end
?if gift_holder("memory") != ""
  {name(gift_holder("memory"))} recites the whole of the Valère letter book, in order, from the first page to the last, with the dates, and stops only when you tell {pg(gift_holder("memory"), "him", "her", "them")} to.
?end
?if gift_holder("healing") != ""
  {name(gift_holder("healing"))} takes a splinter out of the stable boy's hand, and the boy never feels it, and is told to go to bed.
?end
You know your own gifts, of course, and you do not show them. There is a limit, even in a family, to what is done in a courtyard, and the old blood is the family's, and not the city's.
Then, since it is a house that has to be run and not merely admired, it is asked which of the gifts the family should work at, this season, so that they will be sharper by the time that they are wanted.
:the_gifts
* Strength, for the night that comes to the door ?if gift_holder("strength") != ""
  $ b4.gift_focus = "strength"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  They train in the long gallery until the lamps burn low. It is not a gentle thing to watch. It is, you notice, a good deal like a family at a game.
* Charm, for the rooms where a voice is the only door ?if gift_holder("charm") != ""
  $ b4.gift_focus = "charm"
  $ standing += 1
  They practice on each other first, and then on the cook, who has begun to be very careful what she says yes to.
* Shadows, for the streets and the stairs ?if gift_holder("shadows") != ""
  $ b4.gift_focus = "shadows"
  $ secrecy += 1
  They go out in pairs, and come back with a number of things that were not meant to be taken, and a number of things that were not meant to be seen.
* The merchant's eye, for the Exchange and the books ?if gift_holder("merchants_eye") != ""
  $ b4.gift_focus = "merchants_eye"
  $ standing += 1
  They sit at the Exchange in turn, under the dome, among the top hats, and by the end of the week they can tell you which of the planters is about to be ruined, three days before the planter.
* Memory, for the letters and the secrets ?if gift_holder("memory") != ""
  $ b4.gift_focus = "memory"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  They read everything. They are given the Valère letter book, and the newspapers, and the lists of the Exchange, and by the end of the month there is nothing that has passed through the house that they do not know.
* Healing, for the wounded and the hunted ?if gift_holder("healing") != ""
  $ b4.gift_focus = "healing"
  $ humanity += 1
  The Tremé hears of it in a week. It is not meant to. A physician comes to the back door with a very polite note and a man on a hurdle, and does not ask what the house is.
* Let them be as they are
  $ b4.gift_focus = "none"
  They go back to their work, relieved, and a little disappointed, in the way of people who have been shown a thing and then not asked for it.
<~

# treme
## The Faubourg
$ do chart("neworleans.treme")
$ do meet("armand")
$ do discover("the_free_people_of_color")
The Tremé is a long bright sleepy suburb of low cottages, in a hundred colors, with galleries and gardens and a church at the end of every other street. It is the great quarter of the free people of color, who are tradesmen, and builders, and teachers, and a good many of whom are richer than the people who despise them. There are a great many lamps still lit. It is a place where people are awake because they have a good deal to be awake about.
Dr. Armand's house is a long green cottage, with a brass plate. He is in his shirt sleeves, in his surgery, with a lamp, and a very small child on the table with a very large fever, and a mother in the corner, who has said nothing in an hour. He looks up when you come in, and sees what you are, or sees enough, with the quick wide look of a man who has read a great deal. He does not stop what he is doing. He says, in French, very levelly, that he will be a few minutes and that there is a chair.
?if b4.doctor == "helped"
  He says, as he works, that he has not forgotten the cabin, and the lamp, and the good broth that was not drunk, and that he is not a man who forgets a kindness, and that it is a great nuisance to him.
?elif b4.doctor == "talked"
  He says that he has been thinking about what you said on the deck, about home, and has come to the conclusion that you were right and that he does not like it.
?end
When the fever has broken, a little before the dawn, he washes his hands, and sits down in the other chair, and says that his grandfather's friend, in Santiago, a long time ago, was a boy of a house called Valère. "I know a little of what you are," he says. "I know what my family were told. It is not very much, and it is in a poor sort of Latin."
He tells you of the city. He tells it very quietly, in the order of a physician's notes. A law was made in 1857, that no one may free a slave by will or deed, or by any means short of the legislature. A law has been proposed, in this session, that every free man of color shall choose a master or leave the state. There is a society in the Tremé, which has a name that he will not say, which buys people out, one at a time, and sets them to work, and keeps the papers in a very safe place. It has never had enough money. It has never had a friend who was awake at night.
?if b4.auction == "bought"
  He says, with a little hesitation, that there is a woman in a house on the Rue Royale, with a boy, and that it has been remarked in the Tremé, and that the Tremé would like to know what she is to be to the man who has her. It is said with great courtesy. It is also said with the whole of his back.
?elif b4.auction == "stood"
  He says that a woman and her son were sold from a yard on Baronne Street, in January, to a house in the Garden District, and that he has been told that she is well, and that she is a cook, and that the boy has been put to the stables. He says it without any feeling in his voice, and it is the absence of the feeling that you hear.
?else
  He says that there were a great many sold from the yard on Baronne Street in January, and that the Tremé has a list, and that nobody on it has been a name for very long.
?end
:the_doctor
* Say that you will help, in whatever way he asks, and ask what that is
  $ b4.armand = "ally"
  $ humanity += 2
  $ standing += 1
  He looks at you for a long moment, with his hands folded, and says that he does not know. It is the honest answer. It is, he says, the first time in a year that anyone with money has asked him the question. He says that he will have to talk to some other persons, who will not at all like it, and that you are to expect a note.
* Offer him money, quietly, and ask for nothing in return
  $ b4.armand = "patient"
  $ do earn(-4)
  $ humanity += 1
  He takes it as a man takes a thing that he has been told is a gift and knows to be a loan. He says that he is obliged to you, and that he is going to count it, and that you will be told, to the penny, where every part of it has gone. He does not smile. It is evident that this is, for him, the highest form of courtesy.
* Say that the city is dangerous for you both, and that you can promise nothing
  $ b4.armand = "distant"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ humanity -= 1
  He nods, once, as at a diagnosis that he has expected. "That is fair," he says, and it is perfectly civil, and he sees you to the door, and it is a long cold way down the walk. You hear the door close behind you with the softness of a man shutting a book.
<~

# bayou
## The Bayou St. John
$ do chart("neworleans.market")
The shell road goes out of the city to the north, white in the dark, between ditches full of frogs, and at the end of it the bayou lies like a long black mirror under the cypress. There are boats at a landing. There is a light on the far side. It is the only light there is, and it is, evidently, meant to be seen.
The house is a low raised cottage in the old French style, with a double roof and a gallery, and a garden full of roses and four dogs. There is a lamp in the window. The door is not locked. A girl of about sixteen, in a clean white apron, opens it, looks at you carefully, and goes away without a word. A little later a very small old woman in gray silk comes down the stair on a stick, with a boy beside her, who is carrying a candle with the most perfect gravity.
$ do meet("lachapelle")
"Madame Célestine Lachapelle," she says. "You will be the sixth. I have been told." Her French is the French of a century before, the French of the old colony, and her eyes are bright and black and a great deal too shrewd. "You have been to supper with Augustin. It is not a thing that you are to be ashamed of. We have all been to supper with Augustin."
She gives you a chair, and coffee, and a seat by a fire that has been lit for the purpose. There are six in the house, she says. It was nine. There were three that she was not able to keep, and she does not say what happened to them. She was the Beaumont's equal, once, in the days when the river houses were three and not seven. They quarreled, over a matter of principle that has been a great deal discussed and is a very small matter, which is the people. She holds that a house that feeds upon a country should not also own it. He holds that the thing is a good deal older than either of them.
There are, in the back rooms of the cottage, a good many people who are not vampires. They are in the kitchen, and the loft, and the room over the stable, at all times of the day. Some of them are free. Some of them are not, any longer, in any sense that would be recognized by a court. She does not say this, and she does not hide it. A dog yawns, on the rug, and the boy with the candle sits down very quietly beside it.
"I have not signed the Compact in eleven years," she says. "He has not yet found a form of words that would let him say that I have broken it. He is looking. I thought that you should know that, before you have found yourself in a room with it." She smiles. "It is a good deal of trouble to be a person who is always in the right."
:the_bayou
* Hear her out, and promise nothing
  $ b4.rival = "heard"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  She tells it all, in the dry old voice, over the coffee, and you listen, and say nothing at all, and at the end of it she says, "That is wise, and I am disappointed," and the two things are said with a perfectly equal courtesy. You are shown out through the roses. The dogs watch you go. You have a feeling that you have been given a great deal more than you have taken.
* Make an understanding with her, quietly, against the day it is wanted
  $ b4.rival = "allied"
  $ standing -= 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ humanity += 1
  She is not at all surprised. She says that there is a boat at the landing, and that it will be kept ready for anyone you wish to send, at any hour, and that the people in her back rooms can be sent a long way by water. She says that there is nothing written down, and that there never has been, and that this is the reason that she is old. It is a small matter. You know, as you go back along the shell road with the frogs shouting, that you have just done the most dangerous thing in the evening.
* Thank her, and say that you will be speaking of it to Monsieur Beaumont
  $ b4.rival = "told"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  She does not move. She looks at you for a long time, with a face that has no unkindness in it at all. "Yes," she says. "It is what I should have done, in your place, at your age. It is also exactly what he wants." She rings a small bell, and the girl comes, and shows you out through the hall, and the door is not shut behind you, and nobody locks it, and you are aware, until you are at the road, of a small boy's eyes at a window.
<~

# office
## Colline et Compagnie
$ do chart("neworleans.bank")
The firm has a door. It is a green door, on the Rue de la Douane, between a ship chandler and a notary, with a brass plate that Aurélien had made in January and has been polishing ever since in a state of mounting anxiety. It has three rooms and a vault and a brass lamp, and a clerk with a green shade, and an apprentice. It has not, in any real sense, a single customer.
?if b4.office != "opened"
  You turn the key. It is a thing that you have done a very great many times, in a great many cities, with a great many keys.
?end
Rochefort has come in, in spite of the hour, in a coat that he has put on over his nightshirt, because he cannot, he says, be easy in his mind. He has the books. They are good books. He is an honest man and a competent one and he is, you perceive, a little afraid of you in the way that a man is afraid of a cat that has been very quiet.
?if b4.auction == "bought"
  There is a woman at the end of the table, with a bandana and a clean apron, who has been brought in by Aurélien because she can read and cipher, and write a hand like copperplate, and has not once been asked to. She is Philomène. She does not look at you. A small boy is asleep under the table with his head on a ledger. When you come in, she rises, and stands with her eyes lowered and her hands at her sides, and the boy wakes and looks at you with great grave attention, and asks if you are a ghost.
  There are papers on the table that you have been trying not to look at. There is a bill of sale. It is, in the law of the state of Louisiana, a deed of the possession of two persons. It has your name on it. It is a thing that you will not be able to undo with a signature, since the law says that you may not, and you stand and look at it a long time.
?end
:the_firm
* Open the firm in the open, in your own alias, with a notice in the papers
  $ b4.office = "opened"
  $ standing += 2
  $ secrecy -= 1
  The notice goes in the *Picayune* and the *Bee* and the Valère paper on Monday, in a very small and very correct type. It says that Colline et Compagnie, of Paris and New Orleans, shipping and exchange, has opened its offices at the Rue de la Douane. It is the oldest sort of announcement. Within a week there are cards on the tray from men who have never heard of you, and by the end of the month there are a great many of them.
* Share the firm with the Valère, and keep your own name out of it
  $ b4.office = "shared"
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  You say that the firm is the Valère's, and that you are a sleeping partner in the most literal sense, and that your name is to appear nowhere but on a single piece of paper in a single drawer. Aurélien is moved nearly to tears, and Rochefort looks at the ledger with the face of a man who has been given an enormous responsibility and a quite unnecessary amount of freedom.
* Shut it for the present, and think
  $ b4.office = "none"
  $ secrecy += 1
  The brass lamp goes out. Rochefort goes home, relieved, and Aurélien stays behind, a little smaller than before, and says, in a voice that tries to be level, that it is entirely a matter for you.
?if b4.auction == "bought"
  Philomène has not moved. "Monsieur," she says at last, in a very low voice, "the boy will be put out of the way. If it is to be a hard night." It is the first thing that she has said to you. It is exactly the thing that a person says who has learned that it is the way to survive. You say, as gently as you are able, that nobody is going to be put out of the way, and that she is to go home, and that you will see her in the morning. She goes. She does not believe you. You do not see why she should.
?end
<~

# dawn
## The letter
The light comes up gray over the river, and the frogs stop all at once, as if at a signal. You go up the stair of the Rue Royale, in the smell of coffee and the first of the drays, and you find, on the table in your room, where there was nothing at midnight, a letter.
It has been in a locked room, with a shuttered window and a bolted door, and the key has not left your pocket. It is a small, neat, ironical hand.
?if b4.crane_reply == "answered"
  *My dear Colline. I have your note. Very civil, and very much shorter than I had hoped. I am told that you are in the Crescent City, among the cotton factors and the Creoles, where I am sure that you are a great success. I have learned a thing or two since I wrote. I will not tell you what, because I do not think it is for the post. You will know where to find me. I am where I have always been. Crane.*
?elif b4.crane_reply == "kept"
  *My dear Colline. You did not answer, and I did not suppose that you would. A man does not answer a letter like that one from a ship. I write again, in the hope that you have read it a little more carefully by now. They are very much alive here, in the North, in the winter of this year, and they are not in a good temper. I think that you would find the air of New York agreeable. Crane.*
?else
  *My dear Colline. You burned my first, I imagine. It is what I should have done. It is a pity, since it had a good deal of my best prose in it. I enclose a second, which has less. The Houses of the South are, I am told, very fine. They are also, I am told, sitting on a keg. I thought that you might like to know that there are other persons in this country who are aware of it. Crane.*
?end
There is a postscript, in pencil, in a much smaller hand. *Ask Beaumont what he does with the ones he cannot persuade.*
You read it twice, and put it in the stove, and watch it curl. From the street comes the sound of a bell, and a milk cart, and a man selling hot rice cakes in a voice like a violin. In the courtyard, under the banana, the old aunt is sitting in the first gray light with her coffee, and has taken a seat, you notice, where she can see the gate.
$ do rest()
